  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "To which of the following sets do all the positive integers belong?\n\n- $N$\n- ${R}^{+}$\n- ${Q}^{-}$\n- ${Z}^{-}$",
  "worked_solution": "The positive integer numbers are $1,2,3,\u2026$\n\n- Since all positive integer numbers are natural numbers, all the positive integers belong to $N.$ $\\,\u2713$\n\n- Since all positive integer numbers are positive real numbers, all the positive integer numbers belong to ${R}^{+}.$ $\\,\u2713$\n\n- The set ${Q}^{-}$ contains all the negative rational numbers. However, positive integer numbers as, for instance, $2$ don't belong to ${Q}^{-},$ so ${Q}^{-}$ does *not* contain the positive integer numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\n- The set ${Z}^{-}$ contains all the negative integer numbers. However, positive integer numbers as, for instance, $2$ don't belong to ${Z}^{-},$ so ${Z}^{-}$ does *not* contain the positive integer numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
